  

🐳 Building My First Custom Docker Image with Dockerfile 🚀
 


As We continue our Docker learning journey, I explored one of the most important concepts in containerization: Dockerfiles.

  

📌 What is a Dockerfile?

  

A Dockerfile is a text document that contains a set of instructions used to automatically build a Docker image.

  

Instead of manually installing software and configuring environments every time, we can define everything as code and let Docker build the image for us.

  

✅ Consistent builds

  

✅ Reproducible environments

  

✅ Faster deployments

  

✅ Infrastructure as Code approach
 
 
 Vi Dockerfile and inside the file paste the below commands.
 
 FROM ubuntu

  

MAINTAINER mpariyar4411@gmail.com

  

RUN apt-get update

  

RUN apt-get install nginx -y

  

CMD [“echo”,”Image created”]
 
 save the file by pressing ESC and then Type :wq!
 

  

🏗️ Docker Build Process

  

Dockerfile ➜ Docker Image ➜ Docker Container

  

A Dockerfile acts as a blueprint, an image is the packaged application, and a container is the running instance of that image.

  

📚 Hands-On Learning

  

For practice, I created my own Nginx image using a Dockerfile:

  

🔹 Started with an Ubuntu base image

  

🔹 Updated package repositories

  

🔹 Installed Nginx
 

🔹 Built a custom image using Docker Build

  

Example workflow:

  

docker build -t mynginxpractical.
 

The . tells Docker to use the current directory where the Dockerfile exists. 
 


📝 Image Management

  

After building the image, I learned how to:

  

✅ List available images using docker images

  

✅ Tag an image with a version name

  

✅ Prepare the image for Docker Hub

  

Example:

  

docker tag mynginxpractical   jeevanpariyar/mynginxpractical :latest
 



☁️ Sharing Images with Docker Hub

  

Once the image was ready:

  

🔹 Login to Docker Hub

  

🔹 Push the image to a repository

  

🔹 Verify the image online

  

Commands:

  

docker login
  

docker push <new_image_name>
 

  

This makes the image available from anywhere, allowing developers and systems to pull and run it consistently.
 
 Verify if image is pushed to Dock hub by login into: https://hub.docker.com/
 


💡 Key Takeaways

  

✔️ Dockerfiles automate image creation.

  

✔️ Every image starts with a base image.

  

✔️ Images can be customized for specific application needs.

  

✔️ Docker Hub enables image sharing and collaboration.

  

✔️ Dockerfiles are the foundation of modern CI/CD and cloud-native deployments.
 
 

  

